1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,780
This is pip installing 6 popular packages,

2
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:09,120
FastAPI, pandas, requests,

3
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:11,880
rich, httpx, and pydantic,

4
00:00:12,140 --> 00:00:15,100
about 19 seconds. Now the exact same packages

5
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:17,800
with uv, about 5 seconds,

6
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:20,280
and once uv has them cached,

7
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:22,320
a fresh install takes 5 hundredths of a

8
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:24,640
second, uv replaces pip,

9
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:26,960
venv, pyenv, and pipx with one tool.

10
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:28,380
In the next few minutes,

11
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:31,760
I will set it up from scratch the 2026 way,

12
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:34,420
and if you learned uv from an older tutorial,

13
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:36,820
stay with me, because a few things changed

14
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:38,360
and I will show you what.

15
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:41,420
uv is a single program written in Rust by

16
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:43,400
Astral, the team behind Ruff.

17
00:00:43,660 --> 00:00:44,980
On macOS and Linux,

18
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:47,260
paste this one line into your terminal.

19
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:50,500
On Windows, use this PowerShell line instead.

20
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,100
Homebrew, winget, and even pip install uv

21
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:57,780
work too. Then check it with uv --version.

22
00:00:57,860 --> 00:01:01,480
I am on 0.13.9, the latest release right

23
00:01:01,480 --> 00:01:03,440
now. First surprise,

24
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:05,600
you do not need to install Python separately

25
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:09,100
anymore. uv python install downloads a ready-made

26
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:12,980
Python. Here it fetched Python 3.13 in about

27
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:15,220
3 seconds. No installer,

28
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:18,240
no admin rights. With no version number,

29
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:20,080
you get the newest stable one,

30
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:23,980
3.15. uv python list shows every Python

31
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:26,080
on your machine, including the ones that

32
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:27,580
were already there.

33
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:33,100
And uv run --python 3.14 runs anything on

34
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:34,340
that exact version.

35
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:36,240
Now a real project,

36
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:38,680
uv init weather, here is what changed.

37
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:42,500
Older tutorials show a single main.py file.

38
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:46,020
Since uv 0.12, uv init creates a proper

39
00:01:46,020 --> 00:01:49,360
package, your code lives in src slash weather,

40
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:52,080
and pyproject.toml gets a command entry

41
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:53,680
and a build system.

42
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:56,040
That avoids the classic import headaches

43
00:01:56,040 --> 00:01:58,820
later. If you want the old flat layout,

44
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:02,340
add --no-package. And notice,

45
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:04,760
there is no virtual environment yet,

46
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:06,280
let's add two libraries.

47
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:08,820
uv add requests rich.

48
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:10,560
In about one second,

49
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:12,940
uv created the virtual environment,

50
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,020
picked Python 3.15,

51
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:17,720
resolved 10 packages and installed them.

52
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:20,380
pyproject now lists both dependencies and

53
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:21,680
a new file appeared,

54
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:25,380
uv.lock. The lock file pins the exact version

55
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:28,080
of every package. So your teammate,

56
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:29,920
your server, and you,

57
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:32,800
a year from now, all get the same environment.

58
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:36,120
Commit it to git. uv tree shows who depends

59
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:38,460
on what. Here is a tiny app that fetches

60
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:41,160
the current temperature for three cities

61
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:42,800
and prints a table.

62
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:45,640
To run it, you do not activate anything,

63
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:47,520
just uv run weather.

64
00:02:47,720 --> 00:02:49,640
uv checks the lock file,

65
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:52,260
syncs the environment if needed and runs

66
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:55,500
your code. Cloned a project from GitHub,

67
00:02:55,960 --> 00:02:59,980
run uv sync. 10 packages installed the exact

68
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:01,800
versions from the lock file.

69
00:03:02,020 --> 00:03:03,840
This is my favorite feature,

70
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:05,160
single file scripts.

71
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:07,220
This script needs rich.

72
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:10,380
Normally, that means making a virtual environment

73
00:03:10,380 --> 00:03:13,040
just for one file. Instead,

74
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:17,060
uv add --script writes the dependency into

75
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:19,520
the script itself in this comment block.

76
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:22,780
It is a Python standard PEP 723.

77
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:26,460
Now, uv run hello_rich.py.

78
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:29,580
uv builds a cached throwaway environment

79
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:32,660
and runs it. Send this one file to a friend

80
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:35,360
who has uv. And it just works.

81
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:37,720
Command line tools like Ruff do not belong

82
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:39,080
inside your project.

83
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:42,400
uv tool install ruff installs it once in

84
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:44,300
its own isolated environment.

85
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:45,920
And it works everywhere.

86
00:03:46,180 --> 00:03:48,160
Want to try a tool just once?

87
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:50,780
uvx runs it without installing anything.

88
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:53,520
uvx ruff check and it finds the unused import

89
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:56,620
in my file and even offers to fix it.

90
00:03:56,620 --> 00:03:59,940
Already have a project with a requirements.txt

91
00:03:59,940 --> 00:04:05,940
file. uv add -r requirements.txt moves every

92
00:04:05,940 --> 00:04:08,300
package into pyproject in one go.

93
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:11,620
Or keep your old workflow and just put uv

94
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:16,880
in front. uv pip install -r requirements.txt.

95
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:19,200
That is exactly the command I used in the

96
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:23,260
race. In VS Code, uv's .venv folder sits

97
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:24,960
right inside the project.

98
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:27,060
So, VS Code finds it.

99
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:28,520
Open the command palette,

100
00:04:28,700 --> 00:04:31,060
choose Python: Select Interpreter,

101
00:04:31,220 --> 00:04:33,860
and pick .venv. Quick recap.

102
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:36,120
uv python install for Python,

103
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:39,100
uv init and uv add for projects.

104
00:04:39,220 --> 00:04:43,080
uv run to run, uv add --script for single

105
00:04:43,080 --> 00:04:45,740
files and uvx for tools,

106
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:47,480
one tool instead of five.

107
00:04:47,740 --> 00:04:50,140
In the next video, we use uv to build an

108
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:53,380
MCP server so AI can call your own Python

109
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:56,340
code. Subscribe so you do not miss it.
